  var why={storm:'Large instability (temperature falls fast, dry air at 700 mb), strong moisture at the surface, and a long, clockwise-curving hodograph (strong veering shear) favor supercells.',cap:'Warm, moist air near the ground sits under a warm layer (the inversion), the “cap”. Storms stay suppressed until something lifts air through the cap or it weakens, then they can explode.',fog:'T and Td nearly touch right at the surface and the temperature rises just above it: a shallow stable layer. The hodograph is short, so there is little shear. Expect fog or low cloud, not storms.',flood:'T and Td stay close from the surface to 500 mb: the whole column is moist. The hodograph is short (weak shear), so storms are slow movers dumping heavy rain: a flooding setup.'};
